"""Deterministic port of ``cyberphone/json-canonicalization/testdata/numgen.go``.

The official ES6 number vector is ``es6testfile100m.txt`` — 100 million lines, 4.0 GB
uncompressed. Committing it is absurd and downloading it in CI makes the ES6 conformance
claim depend on a network. Neither is necessary, because upstream publishes the
**SHA-256 of the first N lines** of the deterministically generated file:

===========  ==================================================================  ==========
lines        sha256 of the generated bytes                                        size
===========  ==================================================================  ==========
1 000        ``be18b62b6f69cdab33a7e0dae0d9cfa869fda80ddc712221570f9f40a5878687``  37 967
10 000       ``b9f7a8e75ef22a835685a52ccba7f7d6bdc99e34b010992cbc5864cd12be6892``  399 022
100 000      ``22776e6d4b49fa294a0d0f349268e5c28808fe7e0cb2bcbe28f63894e494d4c7``  4 031 728
1 000 000    ``49415fee2c56c77864931bd3624faad425c3c577d6d74e89a83bc725506dad16``  40 357 417
===========  ==================================================================  ==========

Each line is ``<lowercase hex of the IEEE-754 bits, unpadded>,<expected serialisation>\\n``.
The *expected serialisation* half is produced here by
:func:`trappoint_jcs.canon_v1.es6_number`, so reproducing the published hash is a
statement about **our** serialiser, not about our copy of the file: if a single one of
those thousands of numbers formatted differently, the digest would not match.

``float_sequence`` reproduces the generator exactly — 168 static bit patterns, then 2000
consecutive bit patterns from the smallest normal double upward, then an endless SHA-256
chain reinterpreted as little-endian doubles with zero, NaN and the infinities skipped.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import math
import struct
from collections.abc import Callable, Iterator

__all__ = ["PUBLISHED_DIGESTS", "float_sequence", "generate_lines"]

